<!-- doc/src/sgml/external-projects.sgml -->

 <appendix id="external-projects">
  <title>External Projects</title>

  <para>
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> is a complex software project,
   and managing the project is difficult. We have found that many
   enhancements to <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> can be more
   efficiently developed separately from the core project.
  </para>

 <sect1 id="external-interfaces">
  <title>Client Interfaces</title>

  <indexterm>
   <primary>interfaces</primary>
   <secondary>externally maintained</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   There are only two client interfaces included in the base
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> distribution:
   <itemizedlist>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      <link linkend="libpq">libpq</link> is included because it is the
      primary C language interface, and because many other client interfaces
      are built on top of it.
     </para>
    </listitem>

    <listitem>
     <para>
      <link linkend="ecpg">ECPG</link> is included because it depends on the
      server-side SQL grammar, and is therefore sensitive to changes in
      <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> itself.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </itemizedlist>

   All other language interfaces are external projects and are distributed
   separately. A
   <ulink url="https://wiki.postgresql.org/wiki/List_of_drivers">list of language interfaces</ulink>
   is maintained on the PostgreSQL wiki. Note that some of these packages are
   not released under the same license as <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>.
   For more information on each language interface, including licensing terms,
   refer to its website and documentation.
  </para>

  <para>
    <ulink url="https://wiki.postgresql.org/wiki/List_of_drivers"></ulink>
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="external-admin-tools">
 <title>Administration Tools</title>

  <indexterm>
   <primary>administration tools</primary>
   <secondary>externally maintained</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   There are several administration tools available for
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>. The most popular is
   <application><ulink url="https://www.pgadmin.org/">pgAdmin</ulink></application>,
   and there are several commercially available ones as well.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="external-pl">
  <title>Procedural Languages</title>

  <indexterm>
   <primary>procedural language</primary>
   <secondary>externally maintained</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> includes several procedural
   languages with the base distribution: <link
   linkend="plpgsql">PL/pgSQL</link>, <link linkend="pltcl">PL/Tcl</link>,
   <link linkend="plperl">PL/Perl</link>, and <link
   linkend="plpython">PL/Python</link>.
  </para>

  <para>
   In addition, there are a number of procedural languages that are developed
   and maintained outside the core <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>
   distribution. A list of
   <ulink url="https://wiki.postgresql.org/wiki/PL_Matrix">procedural languages</ulink>
   is maintained on the PostgreSQL wiki. Note that some of these projects are
   not released under the same license as <productname>PostgreSQL</productname>.
   For more information on each procedural language, including licensing
   information, refer to its website
   and documentation.
  </para>

  <para>
   <ulink url="https://wiki.postgresql.org/wiki/PL_Matrix"></ulink>
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="external-extensions">
 <title>Extensions</title>

  <indexterm>
   <primary>extension</primary>
   <secondary>externally maintained</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> is designed to be easily extensible. For
   this reason, extensions loaded into the database can function
   just like features that are built in. The
   <filename>contrib/</filename> directory shipped with the source code
   contains several extensions, which are described in
   <xref linkend="contrib"/>.  Other extensions are developed
   independently, like <application><ulink
   url="https://postgis.net/">PostGIS</ulink></application>.  Even
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> replication solutions can be developed
   externally. For example, <application> <ulink
   url="https://www.slony.info">Slony-I</ulink></application> is a popular
   primary/standby replication solution that is developed independently
   from the core project.
  </para>
 </sect1>

 <sect1 id="external-fdw">
  <title>Foreign Data Wrappers</title>

  <indexterm>
   <primary>foreign data wrappers</primary>
   <secondary>externally maintained</secondary>
  </indexterm>

  <para>
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> ships
   <link linkend="postgres-fdw">postgres_fdw</link> and other foreign data
   wrappers as part of <xref linkend="contrib"/>, while many wrappers for
   other remote systems are developed and maintained as external projects.
  </para>

  <sect2 id="external-fdw-oracle-fdw">
   <title>oracle_fdw</title>

   <para>
    <application><ulink
    url="https://github.com/laurenz/oracle_fdw">oracle_fdw</ulink></application>
    is a foreign data wrapper that provides read and write access from
    <productname>IvorySQL</productname> to a remote
    <productname>Oracle</productname> database, including predicate,
    projection and join pushdown and transactional
    <command>INSERT</command>, <command>UPDATE</command> and
    <command>DELETE</command>.  It is an external project: it is not part of
    the IvorySQL distribution, is built against the IvorySQL server headers
    with <application>PGXS</application>, and is not covered by the IvorySQL
    test suite by default.
   </para>

   <para>
    oracle_fdw is listed in the IvorySQL core compatibility allow list
    (<literal>PgDialectExtensions</literal>), which means that in a cluster
    initialized in Oracle mode, <command>CREATE EXTENSION oracle_fdw</command>
    executes the extension's install script under the forced
    <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> dialect and pins the functions the
    script creates to that dialect.  The calling session's
    <varname>ivorysql.compatible_mode</varname> and
    <varname>search_path</varname> are restored after the installation.
    Both entry points of an oracle-mode cluster &mdash; the regular
    <varname>port</varname> and the Oracle listener port
    (<varname>ivorysql.port</varname>) &mdash; can be used to create and use
    oracle_fdw foreign tables.
   </para>

   <para>
    Building oracle_fdw requires Oracle Call Interface (OCI) headers and
    libraries, for example from a regular Oracle Database installation.
    Neither the OCI files nor the oracle_fdw binaries are shipped with
    IvorySQL; they must be provided by the environment.  A typical build and
    installation looks like:
<programlisting>
make PG_CONFIG=<replaceable>/path/to/ivorysql/bin/pg_config</replaceable> \
     ORACLE_HOME=<replaceable>/path/to/oci-home</replaceable> install
</programlisting>
    The Oracle client libraries must also be findable by the server at
    runtime (for example via <envar>LD_LIBRARY_PATH</envar> or the
    equivalent system configuration).
   </para>

   <para>
    A foreign table maps to a remote table with
    <link linkend="sql-createtable"><command>CREATE FOREIGN TABLE</command></link>
    <literal>OPTIONS (schema '<replaceable>SCHEMA</replaceable>', table
    '<replaceable>TABLE</replaceable>')</literal>, and the column list must
    use data type mappings that oracle_fdw supports.  Use the standard
    <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> types for foreign table columns;
    when defining foreign tables from a session connected through the Oracle
    port, spell type names that Oracle mode resolves to
    <productname>IvorySQL</productname>'s Oracle-compatible types (for
    example <type>timestamp</type>) in their qualified standard form
    (<type>pg_catalog.timestamp</type>), because unmodified oracle_fdw only
    converts the standard <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> types.
    Mark the primary key column(s) with
    <literal>OPTIONS (key 'true')</literal> so that <command>UPDATE</command>,
    <command>DELETE</command> and write conflicts are handled correctly.
   </para>

   <para>
    Each database user that accesses the remote database needs its own
    <link linkend="sql-createusermapping">user mapping</link> with the remote
    credentials, and needs <literal>USAGE</literal> on the foreign server
    plus the appropriate table privileges; do not grant these rights to
    <literal>PUBLIC</literal>.  The remote Oracle user should be a dedicated
    account with only the privileges it needs.  Because Oracle treats empty
    strings as <literal>NULL</literal>, empty strings written through
    oracle_fdw arrive as <literal>NULL</literal> on the Oracle side, and
    <command>EXPLAIN VERBOSE</command> on oracle_fdw plans additionally
    requires <literal>SELECT</literal> privileges on
    <literal>V$SQL</literal> and <literal>V$SQL_PLAN</literal> in the remote
    database.
   </para>

   <para>
    To upgrade oracle_fdw, build and install the new version over the old
    one and run <command>ALTER EXTENSION ... UPDATE</command> in every
    database that uses it, following the instructions of the oracle_fdw
    project.
   </para>
  </sect2>
 </sect1>
</appendix>
